Here’s a clean step-by-step with commands to create the IAM role (IRSA) and install the EBS CSI driver add-on on EKS using eksctl + awscli.

I’ll assume you already have: aws, kubectl, and eksctl configured and your EKS cluster is running.

0. Set some variables
export CLUSTER_NAME=my-cluster       # <-- change
export AWS_REGION=ap-south-1         # <-- change to your region

1. Enable IAM OIDC provider for the cluster (required for IRSA)
eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \
  --cluster ${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --region ${AWS_REGION} \
  --approve


This links your cluster to an IAM OIDC provider so service accounts can assume IAM roles. 
AWS Documentation
+1

2. Create IAM Role for EBS CSI driver (IRSA)

AWS has a managed policy AmazonEBSCSIDriverPolicy that we’ll attach to a role using eksctl create iamserviceaccount. 
AWS Documentation

export ROLE_NAME=AmazonEKS_EBS_CSI_DriverRole

eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --name ebs-csi-controller-sa \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --cluster ${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --region ${AWS_REGION} \
  --role-name ${ROLE_NAME} \
  --role-only \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonEBSCSIDriverPolicy \
  --approve


This command does three things:

Creates an IAM role ${ROLE_NAME}.

Configures the trust policy so that the ebs-csi-controller-sa service account in kube-system can assume it (IRSA).

Doesn’t actually create the Kubernetes service account yet because of --role-only – that will be handled by the add-on. 
AWS Documentation

Now grab the role ARN (needed in next step):

aws iam get-role --role-name ${ROLE_NAME} \
  --query 'Role.Arn' --output text


Save that output as:

export SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ROLE_ARN=<output-from-above>

3. Install the aws-ebs-csi-driver EKS add-on
Option A – via eksctl (nice and simple)
eksctl create addon \
  --name aws-ebs-csi-driver \
  --cluster ${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --region ${AWS_REGION} \
  --service-account-role-arn ${SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ROLE_ARN} \
  --force


--force is handy if you re-run the command or had a previous install. 
Kubecost
+1

Option B – via aws eks CLI (if you prefer AWS CLI)
aws eks create-addon \
  --cluster-name ${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --region ${AWS_REGION} \
  --addon-name aws-ebs-csi-driver \
  --service-account-role-arn ${SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ROLE_ARN}


If you already had some resources that conflict, add:

  --resolve-conflicts OVERWRITE


Amazon Web Services, Inc.
+1

